nftables: match xt_tcp and xt_udp as Linux does

What: the xtables "tcp" and "udp" matches that an nf_tables rule holds through
nft_compat match a packet's source and destination port ranges, each possibly
inverted, and for tcp its flags under a mask and an option, as Linux's
net/netfilter/xt_tcpudp.c does, instead of matching every packet. A packet of
another protocol never matches; a header too short to read, and a TCP fragment
at offset 8, are dropped, as Linux's hotdrop drops them. The match's info is
taken when it is at least the struct's size and the same once aligned
(XT_ALIGN), as Linux takes it.

Why: iptables-nft before 1.8.8, such as Ubuntu 22.04's 1.8.7, writes
--sport, --dport and --tcp-flags with these matches rather than with payload
comparisons. A placeholder that matched everything turned each such rule into
one for the whole protocol: the dockerd of Ubuntu 22.04's docker.io writes
"-p udp -m udp --dport 53 -j DNAT" and "-p tcp -m tcp --dport <port> -j DNAT"
rules, and in its containers a lookup through Docker's embedded DNS server got
no answer (busybox nslookup in 5 of 5 runs, wget of a container by name in
some).

Verified:
- gVisor's unit tests in its builder container: the new
  TestCompatTCPUDPMatchEvaluation (ports, inversion, flags, an option, another
  protocol, a short header) and TestCompatTCPUDPMatchParse (aligned and
  unaligned info, too short, too long, unknown inverse flags) pass, as does
  the rest of //pkg/tcpip/nftables:nftables_test. The syscall tests that add
  tcp and udp matches with an unaligned info (CompatNoopMatchTests) pass in
  socket_netlink_netfilter_test, 268 tests, on runsc with this patch.
- Docker in an Ubuntu 22.04 container on runsc with --TESTONLY-nftables,
  CAP_NET_ADMIN, CAP_SYS_ADMIN and its storage on a volume: the docker.io
  dockerd 29.1.3 (iptables 1.8.7), installed in the image or during the run,
  ran containers that resolved each other by name (busybox nslookup and wget,
  5 of 5), reached the Internet, published a port to the container's loopback
  and its own address, built an image and deleted files of their image.

